{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 179,
    "dDurationMs": 5791,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Black hole singularities break physics - fortunately,\nthe universe seems to conspire to protect"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5970,
    "dDurationMs": 3130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "itself from their causality-destroying madness."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9100,
    "dDurationMs": 5230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At least, so says the cosmic censorship hypothesis."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14330,
    "dDurationMs": 6910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Only problem is many physicists think it might\nbe wrong, and that naked singularities may"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21240,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exist after all."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28050,
    "dDurationMs": 2410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Strange things happen inside black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30460,
    "dDurationMs": 4390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Space and time switch roles, pathways open\nup to other universes, and in some cases time"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34850,
    "dDurationMs": 1970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "travel becomes possible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36820,
    "dDurationMs": 7370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Causality breaks down, and so the supreme\nsensibleness of physics is badly threatened."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44190,
    "dDurationMs": 5250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And surrounding all of this weirdness is the\nevent horizon: the surface around the central"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 49440,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "singularity where the inward flow of space\nreaches the speed of light, and time freezes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 54640,
    "dDurationMs": 3110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the perspective of the outside universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57750,
    "dDurationMs": 7309,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the most real possible sense, the interior\nof the black hole is its own separate spacetime,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65059,
    "dDurationMs": 2791,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "excised from our universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67850,
    "dDurationMs": 4430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The event horizon sounds dire, but it’s\na blessing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72280,
    "dDurationMs": 4530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Without it, the infinitely dense singularity\nand its surrounding madness is exposed to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76810,
    "dDurationMs": 1780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the outside universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78590,
    "dDurationMs": 3370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We would have what we call a naked singularity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81960,
    "dDurationMs": 5630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This would wreak havoc on our understanding\nof causality, and our precious laws of physics"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87590,
    "dDurationMs": 2419,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would become unhinged."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90009,
    "dDurationMs": 5801,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Given the dire consequences, Roger Penrose\nproposed the Cosmic Censorship Hypothesis"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95810,
    "dDurationMs": 4830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- which basically states that every gravitational\nsingularity must be surrounded by an event"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 100640,
    "dDurationMs": 1410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102050,
    "dDurationMs": 5209,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In an upcoming episode we’re going to look\ndeeper at why many physicists believe the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107259,
    "dDurationMs": 5821,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cosmic censorship hypothesis must be true\n- even without knowing exactly WHY it’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113080,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "true."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114080,
    "dDurationMs": 5390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And we’ll see why other physicists have\nbegun to doubt it’s validity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119470,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But today we’re going to look at the astrophysics\nand see how exactly a naked singularity might"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126130,
    "dDurationMs": 5119,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be formed in the first place, and how the\nuniverse seems to work very hard to protect"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131249,
    "dDurationMs": 1220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "itself from them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132469,
    "dDurationMs": 5961,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So let’s talk about how to dissolve an event\nhorizon, although I would ask you to please"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138430,
    "dDurationMs": 4029,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not try this at home, for the sake of all\nof physics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142459,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "According to the so-called no-hair theorem,\nblack holes can have only three properties"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 148619,
    "dDurationMs": 2861,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- mass, electric charge, and spin."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151480,
    "dDurationMs": 4810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Mass is what makes a black hole a black hole,\nand so the simplest black holes have only"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156290,
    "dDurationMs": 1520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this property."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 157810,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These are Schwarzschild black holes, and with\nonly mass that means they also only have inward-pulling"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164650,
    "dDurationMs": 1140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165790,
    "dDurationMs": 5489,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With nothing to counter the inward flow of\nspace, an event horizon is inevitable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171279,
    "dDurationMs": 5951,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In recent episodes we also explored the rotating,\nor Kerr black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177230,
    "dDurationMs": 5619,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Inside, we found that the rapid rotation has\nspun the point-like singularity into a ring"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182849,
    "dDurationMs": 2070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of infinite density."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184919,
    "dDurationMs": 4121,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The time machine is on the other side of that\nring, by the way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 189040,
    "dDurationMs": 5489,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The same rotation that f orms the ring also\ndrags the fabric of space into a vortex which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194529,
    "dDurationMs": 3740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "counters the inward pull due to the singularity’s\nmass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 198269,
    "dDurationMs": 4530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The result is that we get this region around\nthe singularity where the faster-than-light"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202799,
    "dDurationMs": 2770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inward flow of space is halted."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205569,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s like the eye of the storm, and it’s\nseparated from the surrounding cascade by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 211849,
    "dDurationMs": 2290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the inner event horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214139,
    "dDurationMs": 5281,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As the spin of a Kerr black hole increases,\nthe spacetime waterfall is beaten back, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 219420,
    "dDurationMs": 1549,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so the inner horizon grows."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220969,
    "dDurationMs": 4901,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At a certain very high rotation rate, the\ninner and outer horizons merge - which means"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225870,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they both vanish."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226870,
    "dDurationMs": 4619,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s no longer a region where the inward\nflow of space exceeds light speed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231489,
    "dDurationMs": 6271,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Instead, there’s a smooth run of normal\n- albeit rapidly spinning space all the way"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237760,
    "dDurationMs": 2250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "down to the singularity ring."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240010,
    "dDurationMs": 2299,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The NAKED singularity ring."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242309,
    "dDurationMs": 5780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s a similar situation with the charged\nblack hole - which, absent rotation, is described"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 248089,
    "dDurationMs": 2760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by the Reisner-Nordstrom metric."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250849,
    "dDurationMs": 5170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The presence of electric charge at the central\nsingularity - which point-like in this case"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256019,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- results in a negative pressure that again\nresists the inward pull of gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260659,
    "dDurationMs": 5081,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Reisner-Nordstrom black holes also have an\ninner horizon, interior to which space and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 265740,
    "dDurationMs": 2510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time seem normal-ish."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 268250,
    "dDurationMs": 5139,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The more electric charge you drop into a black\nhole, the larger its inner horizon becomes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 273389,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And just as with the rotating black hole,\nat some point the inner and outer horizons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277909,
    "dDurationMs": 5470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "become one and vanish and you’re left with\na naked singularity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283379,
    "dDurationMs": 4431,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At the tipping point - when the inner and\nouter horizons are right next to each other"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287810,
    "dDurationMs": 5870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- you have what we call an extremal black\nhole - a black hole with the maximum amount"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293680,
    "dDurationMs": 4170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of spin or charge while still having an event\nhorizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297850,
    "dDurationMs": 6270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In both cases, the amount of angular momentum\nor charge you can fit into a black hole before"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 304120,
    "dDurationMs": 3419,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it becomes extremal depends on the mass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307539,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "More mass means more inward gravity, and so\nthe black hole can hold more spin and charge"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312759,
    "dDurationMs": 1880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "before going extremal."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 314639,
    "dDurationMs": 6221,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So that’s how you make an extremal black\nhole - or even a naked singularity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320860,
    "dDurationMs": 3750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just add enough spin or charge to an existing\nblack hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324610,
    "dDurationMs": 4459,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And actually there’s another way to do it\nin the case of the charged black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329069,
    "dDurationMs": 4531,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Normal black holes leak their mass away by\nemitting Hawking radiation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333600,
    "dDurationMs": 4490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That radiation cian be any type of elementary\nparticle - but in the case of the most massive"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 338090,
    "dDurationMs": 2379,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black holes, it’s mostly just photons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340469,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s because the more massive the black\nhole the lower the temperature of the radiation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345129,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In very massive black holes the Hawking radiation\nhas trouble mustering the energy for anything"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 351289,
    "dDurationMs": 1241,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but weak photons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 352530,
    "dDurationMs": 5530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So that means a massive CHARGED black hole\nwill slowly leak away its mass while retaining"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358060,
    "dDurationMs": 1579,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its charge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359639,
    "dDurationMs": 4971,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The outer event horizon will shrink until\nit meets the inner horizon, and again you"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 364610,
    "dDurationMs": 1779,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have an extremal black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 366389,
    "dDurationMs": 4951,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This can’t happen with rotating black holes\nbecause they leak away their angular momentum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371340,
    "dDurationMs": 2650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as well as their mass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 373990,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Once you have an extremal black hole by whatever\nmethod, it lasts for a very, very long time"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 380050,
    "dDurationMs": 1510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- if not forever."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381560,
    "dDurationMs": 3530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hawking radiation is a direct result of there\nbeing an event horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 385090,
    "dDurationMs": 5530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So naked singularities don’t Hawking-radiate,\nand extremal black holes radiate only very"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390620,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "slowly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 391620,
    "dDurationMs": 4150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That leaves us with a strange situation - in\nthe far distant future, even if all particles"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395770,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the universe decay, we may be left with\nonly radiation and these naked, charged singularities."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 401810,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, there we have a few recipes for theoretical\ndisaster."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 407180,
    "dDurationMs": 4870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At first glance, it appears that extremal\nblack holes are certainly possible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 412050,
    "dDurationMs": 4649,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So why shouldn’t it be possible to add a\nlittle more spin or charge to produce true"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416699,
    "dDurationMs": 2190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "naked singularities?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 418889,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The cosmic censorship hypothesis tells us\nthat something will always stop a gravitational"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 423729,
    "dDurationMs": 5521,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "singularity being stripped of its event horizon\n- but it doesn’t tell us the physical mechanism."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 429250,
    "dDurationMs": 2469,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s look at what we know."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 431719,
    "dDurationMs": 3621,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Rotating black holes gain their angular momentum\nfrom things they swallow."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 435340,
    "dDurationMs": 3810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Stuff doesn’t normally fall straight into\na black hole - it spirals in as its orbit"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439150,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "decays."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 440150,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s that orbital angular momentum that\nis fed to the black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 444150,
    "dDurationMs": 4509,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in order for an object orbiting a black\nhole to fall into it, it actually has to lose"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448659,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at least some of that angular momentum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 449659,
    "dDurationMs": 2090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Otherwise it would just keep orbiting forever."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 451749,
    "dDurationMs": 6331,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, if there’s a disk of gas surrounding\nthe black hole like in a quasar, then the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458080,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gas only spirals inwards because angular momentum\nis carried outwards by friction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 462270,
    "dDurationMs": 5049,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By the time the gas reaches the black hole\nit has lost much of the angular momentum it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 467319,
    "dDurationMs": 1820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "started with."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 469139,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The faster a black hole is rotating, the more\nangular momentum that gas has to lose in order"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 474139,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to fall in."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475139,
    "dDurationMs": 4801,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s because space gets dragged around\nthe rotating black hole, giving the gas a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 479940,
    "dDurationMs": 6289,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sort of boost so it can still orbit even with\nvery little of its own angular momentum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 486229,
    "dDurationMs": 4921,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the case of an extremal Kerr black hole\n- one that’s rotating nearly fast enough"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 491150,
    "dDurationMs": 6460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to lose its event horizon - the gas near the\nevent horizon orbits entirely riding on the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 497610,
    "dDurationMs": 6239,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "carousel of frame-dragged state, and has no\nangular momentum of its own."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 503849,
    "dDurationMs": 5900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Therefore just on the verge of becoming extremal,\nthe black hole can’t gain spin from accretion"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 509749,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "anymore."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 510749,
    "dDurationMs": 7210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "More generally, there is no trajectory into\nan extremal black hole that can add angular"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 517959,
    "dDurationMs": 5330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "momentum from the trajectory or the “orbit”\nitself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 523289,
    "dDurationMs": 5391,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "An alternative is to throw in something that’s\nactually spinning itself - so it has intrinsic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 528680,
    "dDurationMs": 1110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "angular momentum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529790,
    "dDurationMs": 5710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s not yet clear whether we can avoid\nthe naked singularity in this case."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 535500,
    "dDurationMs": 5649,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At any rate, our observations of gravitational\nwaves from colliding black holes and various"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 541149,
    "dDurationMs": 5630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other methods for estimate black hole spin\nhas not yet reveals a single black hole with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 546779,
    "dDurationMs": 2391,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a spin high enough to be super-extremal."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549170,
    "dDurationMs": 8710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For charged black holes the situation is in\nsome ways easier, but has its own weirdness."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 557880,
    "dDurationMs": 5410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We don’t actually expect real, astrophysical\nblack holes to retain any significant change."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 563290,
    "dDurationMs": 5390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A charged black hole in the vicinity of any\nmatter would repel like charges and attract"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568680,
    "dDurationMs": 4099,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and swallow opposite charges, and so quickly\nneutralize."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 572779,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But imagine we create a charged black hole\nand isolate it from all other matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 577399,
    "dDurationMs": 3791,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then surely we can just throw charged particles\ninto the black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 581190,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We have to be careful, because those particles\nincrease the mass of the black hole as well"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 585410,
    "dDurationMs": 5830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as the charge - and if the mass increases\ntoo much it won’t go extremal."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 591240,
    "dDurationMs": 7010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But electrons have very tiny masses for comparatively\nlarge charge - just factoring the electrons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 598250,
    "dDurationMs": 5709,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass, it should be easy to send a black hole\nover the extremal limit by feeding it a stream"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 603959,
    "dDurationMs": 2341,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of electrons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 606300,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But here we get to something that seems like\na bit too neat to be a coincidence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 612120,
    "dDurationMs": 3430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As Einstein taught us, mass and energy are\nequivalent."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 615550,
    "dDurationMs": 5250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And there’s an enormous amount of energy\nin the electric field of all those electrons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 620800,
    "dDurationMs": 2740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that you smooshed together into the black\nhole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623540,
    "dDurationMs": 6739,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact the field itself will always generate\nenough mass to prevent the black hole from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 630279,
    "dDurationMs": 2521,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "losing its event horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 632800,
    "dDurationMs": 6719,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Once again, the universe appears to have a\nmechanism to avoid the naked singularity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 639519,
    "dDurationMs": 7370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there still isn’t an underlying understanding\nof why - or even if - cosmic censorship must"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646889,
    "dDurationMs": 1690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be maintained."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648579,
    "dDurationMs": 5621,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, if you define the cosmic censorship\nhypothesis in more technical terms, it seems"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654200,
    "dDurationMs": 3190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that physics should allow its violation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657390,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that would be a big problem for physics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 659730,
    "dDurationMs": 4390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To really understand that problem, and the\nimplications of the cosmic censorship hypothesis"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 664120,
    "dDurationMs": 7940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "being true or not, you’ll have to wait for\nan upcoming deeper dive to witness the horrors"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672060,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the cosmicly uncensored spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 676300,
    "dDurationMs": 3740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We missed comment responses last week, so\ntoday we’re covering two episodes - building"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 680040,
    "dDurationMs": 6810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black holes in the lab with analog event horizons,\nand Roger Penrose’s conformal cyclic cosmology."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 686850,
    "dDurationMs": 7750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Moksha Prasunadh and k Fitz have related questions\non representing black hles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 694600,
    "dDurationMs": 6290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In representations of black holes as funnels,\nor wormholes as tubes, what does that funnel"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 700890,
    "dDurationMs": 1270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or tube really represent?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 702160,
    "dDurationMs": 4420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Is it, for example, some extra dimension that\nthe black hole leads into?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 706580,
    "dDurationMs": 6610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Actually, in those representations take a\n3-dimensional space and take a two dimensional"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713190,
    "dDurationMs": 7420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "slice out of it, so the black hole or wormhole\nends are circular instead of spherical."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 720610,
    "dDurationMs": 5279,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then the third dimension is just a way to\nrepresent the strength of the spatial curvature"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725889,
    "dDurationMs": 2620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the connections between different regions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 728509,
    "dDurationMs": 4291,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the case of the black hole funnel - moving\ntowards the bottom means moving towards the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 732800,
    "dDurationMs": 7339,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "central singularity, and the narrowing of\nthe funnel represents extremely curved spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740139,
    "dDurationMs": 5390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Each ring you pass as you go down that funnel\nrepresents crossing 3-D spherical shell."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 745529,
    "dDurationMs": 6321,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As you approach the singularity you end up\nbeing wrapped entirely around that shell"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 751850,
    "dDurationMs": 5669,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the case of the tube between wormhole ends,\ntraveling arong the wall of the tube would"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 757519,
    "dDurationMs": 6091,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "feel like traveling through a 3-D space, but\ntravel around the tube and you’d get back"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 763610,
    "dDurationMs": 1900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to where you started."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 765510,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These representations are called embedding\ndiagrams, and we went through them in detial"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 770750,
    "dDurationMs": 3269,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in our recent episode on Wormholes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 774019,
    "dDurationMs": 7870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, on to conformal cyclic cosmology, in which\nthe big bang is hypothesized to be the rescaled"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 781889,
    "dDurationMs": 6611,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "infinite late-time forever of a previous universe,\nleading to a potentially endless chain of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 788500,
    "dDurationMs": 2860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universes, or aeons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 791360,
    "dDurationMs": 7800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "BenoHourglass asks for confirmation of this\ninterpretation of conformal cyclic cosmology:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 799160,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the space between atoms in one aeon would\nbe infinite from the point of view of observers"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 804860,
    "dDurationMs": 2599,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the previous aeon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 807459,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Essentially yes - Each new infinitessimally\nsmall Big Bang corresponds not to just the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 813899,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "very, very large late time of the previous\nuniverse, but actually to the “conformal"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 819019,
    "dDurationMs": 7510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "infinity” of the previous - so all the future\ninfinite time of the previous added together."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 826529,
    "dDurationMs": 6161,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Inyobill asks if we’re assuming that the\nlightest particles are without dimension,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 832690,
    "dDurationMs": 3580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so they have an undefined size relative to\nthe universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 836270,
    "dDurationMs": 3259,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yes - I think that’s the idea."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 839529,
    "dDurationMs": 5341,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A pointlike particle has size zero - that’s\nzero volume, zero radius."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 844870,
    "dDurationMs": 5610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It has the same relative size compared to\nthe universe whether that universe is trillions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 850480,
    "dDurationMs": 2799,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of light years across or a millimeter across."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 853279,
    "dDurationMs": 3850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that’s not enough to make the universe\nscale invariant."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 857129,
    "dDurationMs": 6830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Electrons, for example, supposedly have “zero\nsize”, but they also have something called"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 863959,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an interaction crosssection, which defines\nthe probability of another particle interacting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 868779,
    "dDurationMs": 2620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with the electron as a function of distance."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 871399,
    "dDurationMs": 6341,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s like another type of size, and it\nmakes a big difference if you’re a millimeter"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 877740,
    "dDurationMs": 3250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from an electron versus a trillion light years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 880990,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the case of a universe full of photons\n- I THINK the idea is that when you rescale"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 887470,
    "dDurationMs": 5239,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "both space and time by the same factor, you\nalso rescale the interaction probabilities"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 892709,
    "dDurationMs": 6811,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to that the interaction rate scales in the\nsame way ... So a pair of photons one light-second"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 899520,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "apart have the same probability of interacting\nover one second as do a pair of photons a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 904880,
    "dDurationMs": 3629,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "billion light years apart over a billion years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 908509,
    "dDurationMs": 2770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But I’m going to have to dig deeper on that\none."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 911279,
    "dDurationMs": 3331,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If anyone knows, please shout out in the comments."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 914610,
    "dDurationMs": 5659,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I mentioned that in conformal cyclic cosmology,\nphotons and gravitational waves can pass the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 920269,
    "dDurationMs": 5761,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "boundary from universe end to new big bang,\nand so there may be a way to send messages"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 926030,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "between these universes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 928430,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "MANY of you had a guess at what we might read\nimprinted on the cosmic microwave background"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 933190,
    "dDurationMs": 2830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the previous aeon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 936020,
    "dDurationMs": 1410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some of my favorites:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 937430,
    "dDurationMs": 5699,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“We have been trying to reach you about\nyour car’s expiring warranty”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 943129,
    "dDurationMs": 1900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“Smedjip was here\""
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 945029,
    "dDurationMs": 3980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\"Hey folks, Enjoy your entropy while it's\nlow.\""
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 949009,
    "dDurationMs": 4991,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And a gratifying number of Hitchhikers Guide\nto the Galaxy references:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 954000,
    "dDurationMs": 1649,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\"The answer is 42.\""
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 955649,
    "dDurationMs": 4031,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\"Goodbye and thanks for all the fish.\""
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 959680,
    "dDurationMs": 2190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\"We apologize for the inconvenience\"."
    } ]
  } ]
}
